{
  "image_name": "3a35d8adff9c27827ff75dc6915d117b.png",
  "prompt_text": "This schematic summarizes how different T-cell populations shape anti-tumor immunity and clinical outcome. The upper half depicts a favorable immune environment: abundant infiltrating CD8 T cells attack tumor cells with granzymes and perforin, while producing inflammatory cytokines such as IFNγ and TNF that appear to increase MHC-I and MHC-II expression on the tumor. Naive CD4 cells are shown differentiating toward helper states, including TH1, TH17, and especially T follicular helper cells, which are selectively enriched and signal through CXCR5, CXCL13, and IL-21. These helper interactions support tertiary lymphoid structure formation, with dendritic cells and B cells contributing to B-cell recruitment, activation, and maturation. Stem-like CD8 cells recognizing HPV peptides and responding to anti-PD1 are also shown proliferating and differentiating, suggesting a source of sustained effector responses.\n\nThe lower half contrasts this with an unfavorable suppressive environment dominated by regulatory T cells. A higher Treg-to-effector ratio and lower overall T-cell infiltrates are linked to worse outcome. Tregs inhibit effector cells through IL-10, TGFβ, IL-35, adenosine generation from ATP, and competition for IL-2. Checkpoint blockade, especially anti-CTLA4, is depicted as restoring effector T-cell function by counteracting Treg-mediated suppression.",
